{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n6le03ob07re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n6le03ob07re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob07re2img01.jpg", "text": "<b>Glenn</b>: Hey Elsa! Could you help me understand what switch statements are?</br>\n<b>Elsa</b>: Sure! I was actually studying for our JavaScript class. </br>\n<b>Glenn</b>: Great! I know we can use switch statements instead of the \u2018if\u2026else\u2026if' ones when we have to deal with different conditions that depend on the value of a single variable, right?</br>\n<b>Elsa</b>: That's right. When we want to perform a multiway branch, we may want to use switch statements. This could simplify the decision making process. Basically, the objective of switch statements is to evaluate several statements based on the value of an expression. The program uses an interpreter which goes over each case and compares them with the expression until a match is found. If the interpreter goes over all the cases and finds no match, the default condition will be executed.", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob07re2img02.jpg", "text": "<b>Glenn</b>: Do you think that knowing the syntax may help me understand how switch statements work more easily?</br>\n<b>Elsa</b>: It may. Let me tell you how syntax works. First, we have to write \u2018switch (expression)'. Here, you will write the value of the variable that you are going to be working with. Then, you are going to write the different conditions.</br>\n<b>Glenn</b>: I think this process may be difficult. There are many conditions to take into account. </br>\n<b>Elsa</b>: You can write a list on a piece of paper and then write the code. If you realize you missed a condition, you could edit the code. Make sure you write \u2018break' after you've indicated each condition. This will show where each case ends. Remember that you are going to write the conditions starting with \u2018case', then you'll assign a letter or a number to each case. After this, you will need to indicate the conditions inside a parenthesis. Once you've done this, you'll write a default statement that will be executed if none of the previous conditions matched.", "time": "59000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n6le03ob07re2img03.jpg", "text": "<b>Glenn</b>: If my program shows a default statement, could I have missed a condition?</br>\n<b>Elsa</b>: Maybe, but you will have to go through the code to make sure you didn't miss anything. I want to show you a flowchart that will clarify how switch statements work. As you can see, we first have the expression. Then, the interpreter goes case by case looking for a matching statement. If it finds a matching statement, it'll execute it; if not, the interpreter will evaluate the following statement. This process will go on until the program has found either a matching statement or the default block. </br>\n<b>Glenn</b>: Thank you, Elsa! Everything is much clearer now.", "time": "131000"}]}}], "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "img_path": "", "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can switch statements in JavaScript.   ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can identify information in a flowchart of a switch statement.  ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about the syntax to use in a switch statement in JavaScript.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "How do you think conditions in Javascript work? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Why do you think conditions are important when programming?", "type": "Free Header Item"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2,